Chapter 15
A few hours later Recovery Girl pushed the infirmary door open with her cane and stopped.
Curtis was sitting up. Bandages loose. He held a dark glass wine bottle in one hand and a line running from his arm in the other. His own blood poured into the bottle in a steady stream. He capped it, shook it once like he was mixing a drink, then tipped it back and drank the entire thing. Color returned to his face between swallows. The monitors jumped, then settled.
He lowered the empty bottle, noticed her, and smiled—easy, a little sheepish.
“Oh. Hello there. I’m Curtis Beardy. I don’t think we’ve met yet.”
Recovery Girl stared. The cane tapped once against the floor. Her expression had gone from professional concern to pure, exhausted disbelief.
“…You’re drinking your own blood.”
“Rh null,” he said, like that explained the method. “Couldn’t wait on a donor. This works faster.” He set the bottle on the side table. “Sorry about the mess. And the fight. And the property damage. How’s Mr. Aizawa?”
She closed her eyes for a second, breathed, and opened them again. “Alive. Annoyed. Same as me.” The cane tapped once more. “Lie back down. We’re going to discuss why a student is performing auto-transfusion with a wine bottle. And you’re going to stay in that bed until I say otherwise.”
Curtis nodded, still smiling faintly, and eased back against the pillows. The low, steady pull in his chest was quiet again. Recovery Girl stepped fully into the room and shut the door behind her.
Recovery Girl pulled the chair closer and sat, chart in her lap. “Start with the blood. Rh null isn’t something people casually discover. How long have you known?”
Curtis scratched at the bandage on his arm, thinking. “Not sure I do know. Never had a real doctor’s visit. Grew up in the mountains. If something broke, you wrapped it or you didn’t. First time anyone stuck a needle in me for testing was after I got to Japan.”
Her frown deepened. “And the wine bottle method?”
“Appalachia. Few years back.” He leaned into the pillows. “Got torn up bad by something in the woods. Lost a lot of blood. Found an old cabin with a bottle of red wine still in it. Figured I was dying anyway, so I might as well have a drink.” A short, rough laugh. “Cut was still open. Blood mixed in while I was pouring. Drank it. Woke up with most of the color back and a headache. Been using it ever since when I need to. Works. Tastes awful, though. Can’t stand wine now.”
Recovery Girl was quiet for a moment, pen still. “You self-transfused with contaminated alcohol because you were bleeding out in a forest and had no other option.”
“Pretty much.”
She exhaled through her nose. “And the scar tissue. The old breaks.”
“Same woods. Same kind of nights.” Curtis met her eyes, expression even. “I heal fast once the blood’s back. Doesn’t mean the damage never happened.”
She wrote something on the chart, then looked at him again. “You’re staying here until I confirm that trick of yours isn’t going to kill you later. And we’re finding a proper Rh null supply so you never have to drink another bottle. Understood?”
“Understood.”
The monitors kept their steady rhythm. Recovery Girl didn’t smile, but the worst of the disbelief had eased into something more like grim acceptance.
Curtis waited until Recovery Girl stepped out to update the chart. He dug his phone from the folded uniform on the side table. The screen lit with notifications.
Missed calls: Janice (4). Tatsuya (2). Hina (3).
Then the texts.
Janice:
You alive? News is saying something happened at the USJ. Call me.
Tatsuya:
Kid. Pick up. Manager’s about to drive to the school.
Hina:
Curtis??
There’s a picture on every feed.
Please just tell me you’re okay.
He opened the news link. The headline was vague—U.A. Training Facility Incident, Students Safe, Details Withheld. The photo attached was garbage resolution: a stretcher, Recovery Girl’s silhouette, and the back of a student’s head. Long braids. Unmistakable if you knew what you were looking for.
Curtis stared at it for a second, then typed with his good thumb.
To Janice:
Alive. Infirmary. Can’t make the shift. Sorry.
To Tatsuya:
Tell her not to drive over. I’m fine.
To Hina:
I’m okay. Just banged up. Braids gave me away, huh?
He hit send on the last one and let the phone rest on his chest. The replies started coming almost immediately. He closed his eyes and listened to the monitors while the low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet and the rest of the school kept moving somewhere outside the infirmary walls.
Lumen – Staff Room
Janice, Tatsuya, and Hina sat on the floor in a loose circle, phones face-up between them. The club was dark. No customers. They’d closed early the second the USJ headlines hit.
All three screens lit at once.
Janice read hers first. Her shoulders dropped. “Alive. Infirmary. Can’t make the shift.” She let out a breath that sounded like it had been stuck for hours. “Idiot. Of course he apologized for the shift.”
Tatsuya’s mouth twitched as he scanned his own. “Told her not to drive over. He’s fine.” He glanced at Janice. “You’re still not driving over.”
“I might.”
Hina’s eyes were wet, but she was smiling at her screen. “He said the braids gave him away.” She wiped her cheek with the heel of her hand. “He’s okay. He’s actually okay.”
Janice reached over and squeezed her knee once. “Okay enough to text. That’s better than the picture.” She looked at the pixelated stretcher photo still open on Tatsuya’s phone. “We’re keeping the weekend slot open. And I’m sending food whether he wants it or not.”
Tatsuya nodded. “I’ll drop it. Less chance of you getting arrested for storming a hero school.”
Hina laughed, small and shaky. “Can I come?”
“Yeah,” Janice said. “You can come.”
The three of them stayed on the floor a while longer, phones quiet now, the empty club humming around them while the worst of the night finally loosened its grip.
Back at the infirmary
The infirmary door opened quietly. Midoriya and Uraraka stepped in, still in their school uniforms, both looking like they hadn’t fully settled since the USJ.
“Curtis?” Midoriya’s voice was soft. “Recovery Girl said you were awake. We—we wanted to check on you.”
Uraraka hovered just behind him, cheeks already pink. She held a small bag of fruit. “We brought snacks. If you can eat.”
Curtis shifted against the pillows, bandages pulling. He managed a small smile. “Hey. I’m good. Mostly.” His eyes moved between them. “You two okay? Everybody else?”
“We’re fine,” Midoriya said quickly. “Shaken. But fine. You… you held that thing off. All Might said if you hadn’t—” He stopped, hands clenched. “Thank you.”
Uraraka nodded, eyes bright. “You told us to stay back and then you just… stayed. I was so scared you weren’t going to get up.”
“Got up eventually.” Curtis’s tone stayed light. “Sorry for the scare. And the property damage. Again.”
Midoriya laughed once, wet and relieved. Uraraka set the fruit on the side table and lingered, fingers twisting. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet while the two of them filled the room with careful questions and the simple fact that they’d come.
Midoriya and Uraraka left with promises to check in tomorrow. The door clicked shut. Curtis sat up, swung his legs over the side of the bed, and stood. The worst of the breaks were already knit—Recovery Girl’s quirk and his own accelerated healing had done most of the work.
He crossed to the corner where his costume had been left. The armor sat in its case, fully reformed. No cracks. No missing plates. The cryptid material had stitched itself back together while he slept. He closed the case, dug through his duffel, and pulled on the black compression shirt and pants. The fabric settled tight across his chest and arms. Braids tied back. He looked like he was heading to training instead of leaving an infirmary.
Recovery Girl walked in as he zipped the bag. She stopped. “Goodness. You can’t leave.”
Curtis bowed, short and proper. “Thanks for the treatment. Your quirk healed most of the injuries. I have things to do tonight.”
“What would your parents think?” The words came out automatic, scolding.
He didn’t answer. He picked up the duffel, nodded once more, and walked out.
Aizawa was in the hallway, sling still on, leaning against the wall. He’d heard. His eye followed Curtis down the corridor, then shifted to Recovery Girl in the doorway.
“Not the best choice of words. Beardy doesn’t have any.”
Recovery Girl’s mouth tightened. She watched the tall figure disappear around the corner, compression clothes and all, and didn’t call after him. Aizawa pushed off the wall and limped the other way. The infirmary door swung shut on an empty bed.
Recovery Girl stood in the doorway a moment longer, cane resting against her shoulder. The scolding expression had drained into something quieter. She looked at the empty bed, then at Aizawa.
“I didn’t know.”
“Most don’t,” Aizawa said. He shifted his weight off the bad leg. “He mentioned it in class when he resigned as representative. No parents. Grew up alone. Doesn’t dress it up.”
She exhaled. “And I just asked him what they’d think.” The cane tapped once, soft. “He drank his own blood out of a wine bottle because he learned the trick while bleeding out in a forest. No formal medical history until he reached us. And he still walked out of here like the bed was optional.”
Aizawa’s eye stayed on the direction Curtis had gone. “He’ll be at that club or a net café by midnight. I’ve already told Nezu. We’ll keep the blood request active and make sure someone checks that he actually sleeps.”
Recovery Girl nodded, tired. “Tell the class he’s stable if they ask. And Aizawa—” She glanced at his sling. “You’re not far behind him on the ‘should still be in bed’ list.”
“I’m aware.” He pushed off the wall. “I’ll handle the report. You handle the next idiot who tries to leave early.”
She huffed, almost a laugh, and turned back into the infirmary. Aizawa limped toward the faculty office, the corridor quiet again except for the distant sound of students still waiting on news.
